`<code>` is inside a `<pre>` tag. The simplest approach is to use a regex that matches `<pre>...<code...>...</code>...</pre>` blocks.\n\nActually, for a more robust approach, I can use a simple state machine or regex. Let me think about regex:\n\n```typescript\ncontent = content.replace(/<pre>([\\s\\S]*?)<\\/pre>/g, (match, preContent) => {\n  // Now process code blocks within pre content\n  return `<pre${modifiedAttributes}>${modifiedPreContent}</pre>`;\n});\n```\n\nInside `<pre>`, I need to find `<code class=\"language-xxx\">` patterns and:\n1. Replace the class to add `highlighted`\n2. Add `data-language=\"xxx\"` to the `<pre>` tag\n\nHmm, but with a single regex pass, modifying the `<pre>` tag and its content simultaneously is tricky. Let me use a different approach:\n\n```typescript\ncontent = content.replace(/(<pre)([\\s\\S]*?)(<\\/pre>)/g, (match, preOpen, inner, preClose) => {\n  // Find a code block with language class\n  const codeMatch = inner.match(/<code\\s+class=\"language-(\\w+)\"/);\n  if (!codeMatch) return match; // No language, return unchanged\n  const lang = codeMatch[1];\n  // Add data-language to pre tag and highlighted to code block\n  inner = inner.replace(/<code\\s+class=\"language-\\w+\"/, `<code class=\"language-${lang} highlighted\"`);\n  return `${preOpen} data-language=\"${lang}\"${inner}${preClose}`;\n});\n```\n\nWait, but if content has newlines and the `<pre>` tag might span multiple lines, I need `[\\s\\S]*?` which is fine.\n\nLet me trace through:\nInput: `<pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">const x = 1;</code></pre>`\n- `preOpen` = `<pre`\n- `inner` = `><code class=\"language-javascript\">const x = 1;</code>`\n- `preClose` = `</pre>`\n- `codeMatch` = captures `javascript`\n- `inner` becomes `><code class=\"language-javascript highlighted\">const x = 1;</code>`\n- Return: `<pre data-language=\"javascript\"><code class=\"language-javascript highlighted\">const x = 1;</code></pre>`\n\nThat looks correct! But wait, the test checks for `class=\"language-javascript highlighted\"` (order). Let me verify my regex preserves the order correctly.\n\nThe input has `class=\"language-javascript\"`. My replacement is:\n```\n<code class=\"language-${lang} highlighted\"\n```\nWhich gives `class=\"language-javascript highlighted\"`. Good.\n\nBut wait, what about attributes on `<pre>` that might already exist? The tests don't have any pre-existing attributes on `<pre>`, so I'll keep it simple.\n\n### 4. taxonomy.ts\n\nThis plugin:\n- `beforeBuild`: Builds tag index from file tags, stores in `ctx.data` as `taxonomy-tags` (Map<string, ContentFile[]>)\n- `afterBuild`: Generates tag listing pages in `ctx.output` as `tags/<tag>.html`\n- Tags are case-normalized to lowercase\n- Files with no tags are skipped\n- Empty file list handled\n\nThe tag page content should contain the file titles that have that tag.\n\nLooking at the test:\n```javascript\nexpect(ctx.output.get('tags/javascript.html')).toContain('Post 1');\n```\n\nSo the tag page should just contain the titles of files with that tag. It's a simple listing page.\n\n### 5. rss-feed.ts\n\nThis plugin generates RSS 2.0 XML in `ctx.output` under `feed.xml`.\n\nRequirements from tests:\n- Valid RSS 2.0 XML with `<?xml version=\"1.0\"`, `<rss version=\"2.0\">`, `<channel>`, `<item>`, `</rss>`\n- Item content wrapped in `<![CDATA[...]]>`\n- Category tags from taxonomy (if `taxonomy-tags` in ctx.data)\n- Channel title from config title\n- Items sorted by date newest first\n- Files without dates handled (should still appear but no `<pubDate>undefined</pubDate>`)\n- Feed link uses baseUrl from config\n- Item link: `{baseUrl}/{slug}.html`\n\nFor the CDATA content, the test expects:\n```html\n<![CDATA[<p>This is <strong>HTML</strong> content</p>]]>\n```\n\nFor tags, the test checks for:\n```xml\n<category>javascript</category>\n```\n\nI'll generate the XML as a string (no external XML library needed).\n\n### 6. search-index.ts\n\nThis plugin generates `search-index.json` in `ctx.output`.\n\nRequirements:\n- JSON array of objects with `slug`, `title`, `content` (plain text, no HTML), optionally `tags`\n- HTML tags stripped from content\n- Code blocks included in searchable text\n- Empty file list → `[]`\n- Valid JSON\n- Tags included if present\n\nFor stripping HTML, I can use a simple regex: `content.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '')` and then trim.\n\n### 7. sitemap.ts\n\nThis plugin generates `sitemap.xml` in `ctx.output` from content pages and taxonomy pages.\n\nRequirements:\n- Uses `ctx.output` to find all pages (already rendered pages)\n- Includes taxonomy tag page URLs from `taxonomy-tags` in ctx.data \n- Includes `feed.xml` URL\n- URLs use baseUrl from config\n- Valid XML structure with `<urlset>`, `<url>`, `<loc>`\n- Handles site with no tag pages\n\nBut wait - the sitemap includes URLs from ctx.output AND from taxonomy tag pages. The taxonomy tags are generated in the afterBuild hook, which runs before sitemap's afterBuild (if ordering is correct). So the tag pages should already be in ctx.output by the time sitemap runs.\n\nBut actually, looking at the test more carefully:\n```javascript\nit('includes taxonomy tag page URLs', async () => {\n    const output = new Map<string, string>();\n    output.set('tags/javascript.html', '<html>js tag page</html>');\n    const tagMap = new Map<string, ContentFile[]>();\n    tagMap.set('javascript', []);\n    const data = new Map<string, unknown>();\n    data.set('taxonomy-tags', tagMap);\n    const ctx = makeCtx(output, data);\n    await plugin.hooks.afterBuild!(ctx);\n    const sitemap = ctx.output.get('sitemap.xml')!;\n    expect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/tags/javascript.html');\n});\n```\n\nThis test sets up the output with `tags/javascript.html` already in it AND provides the `taxonomy-tags` data. So the sitemap could use either the output map directly (which already contains tag pages from taxonomy plugin), or iterate over the taxonomy-tags data.\n\nThe simplest approach: iterate over `ctx.output.keys()` to get all pages, build sitemap from those. Since tag pages and feed.xml are already in ctx.output by the time sitemap runs (due to dependency ordering), this should work.\n\nActually wait - but the sitemap test for \"includes feed.xml URL\" just feeds in `feed.xml` as an output key. Let me re-check...\n\n```javascript\nit('includes feed.xml URL', async () => {\n    const output = new Map<string, string>();\n    output.set('feed.xml', '<rss></rss>');\n    const ctx = makeCtx(output);\n    await plugin.hooks.afterBuild!(ctx);\n    const sitemap = ctx.output.get('sitemap.xml')!;\n    expect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/feed.xml');\n});\n```\n\nSo it just checks that URLs from ctx.output are in the sitemap, including `feed.xml`. I can just iterate over ctx.output keys.\n\nBut what about the taxonomy test? It both sets the output AND provides data. Let me check if the approach of just iterating over ctx.output works:\n\nThe test sets `output.set('tags/javascript.html', '...')` AND provides `taxonomy-tags` in data. If I just iterate over ctx.output, `tags/javascript.html` would be included. So I don't need to do anything special for taxonomy pages - they're already in the output.\n\nBut wait - is the sitemap supposed to ALSO include the `feed.xml` URL specifically? The test says yes, and if `feed.xml` is in ctx.output (which the rss-feed plugin puts there), iterating over ctx.output keys would include it.\n\nSo the simplest implementation: just iterate over `ctx.output.keys()`, ignore `sitemap.xml` itself, and generate URLs from the keys.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: the sitemap shouldn't include `sitemap.xml` itself in its own URLs. I should skip it.\n\nAlso, the test \"handles site with no tag pages\" checks that `/tags/` is not in the sitemap. If the output doesn't have any tags/ pages, this would naturally not appear.\n\nOK, let me also consider: should I use the `taxonomy-tags` data to add tag page URLs even if they're not in output? Looking at the test:\n\nThe test provides BOTH the output entry AND the taxonomy data. The output entry is what the taxonomy plugin would have generated. So output iteration alone should cover it.\n\n### 8. dev-server.ts\n\nThis plugin stores dev-server-config in ctx.data.\n\nRequirements:\n- `ctx.data` gets `dev-server-config` with:\n  - `port`: number, default 3000\n  - `routes`: array of `{ path: string; handler: string }`\n    - Content pages have handler `'static'`\n    - Asset routes have handler `'asset'`\n  - `assetPaths`: string[] from `asset-manifest` in ctx.data (if present)\n  - `searchIndexPath`: string, `/search-index.json` (if search-index.json exists in output)\n\nLooking at the test for \"config includes asset routes from asset-pipeline\":\n```javascript\nconst config = ctx.data.get('dev-server-config') as DevServerConfig;\nexpect(config.assetPaths).toContain('image.png');\nexpect(config.assetPaths).toContain('style.css');\nconst assetRoutes = config.routes.filter(r => r.handler === 'asset');\nexpect(assetRoutes.length).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(2);\n```\n\nSo each asset gets both an entry in `assetPaths` AND a route with handler 'asset'.\n\nAnd for content routes:\n```javascript\nconst contentRoutes = config.routes.filter(r => r.handler === 'static');\nexpect(contentRoutes.length).toBe(2);\n```\n\nEach output page (excluding search-index.json, sitemap.xml, feed.xml maybe?) gets a static route.\n\nAlso: \"handles missing asset-pipeline data gracefully\" - if no asset-manifest, assetPaths should be `[]`.\n\nOK let me now implement everything. Let me create the tasks and get started.\n\nActually, I'll write the contract first, then implement each plugin test-first. But since the tests already exist, the \"test-first\" part means I need to run the tests to see them fail, then implement the plugin, then run them to see them pass.\n\nLet me start by creating the plugin directory and files.","signature":"d9ce058e-4934-47df-a0a8-4af9015a6be4